1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,234
One list, two colors. Just as promised.

2
00:00:03,384 --> 00:00:06,369
Excellent. Yesterday my shopping needed a
referee.

3
00:00:06,519 --> 00:00:09,503
Today we are all coming with you.

4
00:00:09,653 --> 00:00:14,300
Good. I want to hear how you explain those
colors, Dan.

5
00:00:15,250 --> 00:00:18,454
Can we have our picnic under our tree?

6
00:00:18,604 --> 00:00:21,417
Yes. We can make fruit salad first.

7
00:00:21,567 --> 00:00:25,163
Then our lunch will have a very short journey.

8
00:00:25,313 --> 00:00:29,300
The shade under that tree is just the right
size.

9
00:00:30,250 --> 00:00:33,757
Sixteen dollars for fruit. That is our limit.

10
00:00:33,907 --> 00:00:35,700
But I have twenty.

11
00:00:35,850 --> 00:00:40,214
Four dollars already have another job. Let us
keep them.

12
00:00:40,364 --> 00:00:44,300
Four stays out. Sixteen is what we can spend.

13
00:00:45,250 --> 00:00:48,594
Plates, forks, napkins, water. We have these
already.

14
00:00:48,744 --> 00:00:51,270
Is the blanket on the list?

15
00:00:51,420 --> 00:00:54,356
Yes. Lunch needs somewhere comfortable to sit.

16
00:00:54,506 --> 00:00:56,623
Let's take the blanket, then.

17
00:00:56,773 --> 00:00:59,300
Good. Grass makes my knees itch.

18
00:01:00,250 --> 00:01:04,290
I will choose the biggest fruit in the shop.

19
00:01:04,440 --> 00:01:06,720
Choose fruit we will enjoy.

20
00:01:06,870 --> 00:01:09,590
And fruit we can actually carry.

21
00:01:09,740 --> 00:01:13,780
You're taking all the fun out of showing off.

22
00:01:15,250 --> 00:01:19,356
What if the biggest fruit is bigger than our
basket?

23
00:01:19,506 --> 00:01:21,998
Then the basket will need confidence.

24
00:01:22,148 --> 00:01:25,044
I think it needs a bigger door.

25
00:01:25,194 --> 00:01:29,300
Let's look at the basket before we choose a
winner.

26
00:01:30,250 --> 00:01:33,410
Ella and I will check the fruit.

27
00:01:33,560 --> 00:01:36,280
Max and I can compare tastes.

28
00:01:36,430 --> 00:01:40,030
I will watch the prices. Dad can listen.

29
00:01:40,180 --> 00:01:43,780
Listening. That's a shopping job I haven't
tried.

30
00:01:45,250 --> 00:01:48,850
Six family members. One list. One modest
basket.

31
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:51,720
And one very confident fruit expert.

32
00:01:51,870 --> 00:01:53,710
I appreciate the promotion.

33
00:01:53,860 --> 00:01:57,900
It's a trial position. Let's see what you
choose.

34
00:02:00,250 --> 00:02:02,970
Apples, peaches, strawberries. It smells
wonderful.

35
00:02:03,120 --> 00:02:05,400
And there is the champion!

36
00:02:05,550 --> 00:02:09,590
Let us meet the fruit before we award prizes.

37
00:02:09,740 --> 00:02:14,220
Are we handing out medals before we've seen
the prices?

38
00:02:15,250 --> 00:02:18,410
That watermelon could feed a whole picnic.

39
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:20,400
It costs eight dollars.

40
00:02:20,550 --> 00:02:23,710
It could also be the picnic table.

41
00:02:23,860 --> 00:02:28,340
Half our money on one fruit? That's quite a
table.

42
00:02:30,250 --> 00:02:32,312
It will not fit.

43
00:02:32,462 --> 00:02:33,538
Perhaps diagonally?

44
00:02:33,688 --> 00:02:37,722
Dad, it is already round in every direction.

45
00:02:37,872 --> 00:02:42,400
It does seem to be winning against the basket.

46
00:02:45,250 --> 00:02:48,410
A tiny yellow orange. Finally, something
basket-sized.

47
00:02:48,560 --> 00:02:50,400
It has pointy ends.

48
00:02:50,550 --> 00:02:54,590
Let us check what it is before we choose.

49
00:02:54,740 --> 00:02:58,780
A real orange doesn't usually have that little
nose.

50
00:03:00,250 --> 00:03:03,850
The oranges are rounder. This one looks
different.

51
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:05,840
The label says lemon.

52
00:03:05,990 --> 00:03:09,590
Then my orange is using a different name.

53
00:03:09,740 --> 00:03:13,780
That name might explain why it smells so
different.

54
00:03:15,250 --> 00:03:19,730
We can compare a lemon sample with an orange
sample.

55
00:03:19,880 --> 00:03:21,280
A tiny taste?

56
00:03:21,430 --> 00:03:24,590
If you want one. First, clean hands.

57
00:03:24,740 --> 00:03:28,780
Okay. I want to see Dad's face first, though.

58
00:03:30,250 --> 00:03:33,850
I was rather sure about my tiny orange.

59
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:36,280
You can change your mind.

60
00:03:36,430 --> 00:03:39,150
We came to choose together, remember?

61
00:03:39,300 --> 00:03:43,780
Right. You can all help me pick a better name.

62
00:03:45,250 --> 00:03:48,952
Back you go. I should have asked your name.

63
00:03:49,102 --> 00:03:53,611
The card has both. We can use them for
different things.

64
00:03:53,761 --> 00:03:56,254
So which one should taste sweeter?

65
00:03:56,404 --> 00:03:59,300
Let's find out before you guess again.

66
00:04:00,250 --> 00:04:03,850
Do lemons need us to wash our hands?

67
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:08,040
The food does. We will all take a turn.

68
00:04:08,190 --> 00:04:10,030
Even the fruit expert.

69
00:04:10,180 --> 00:04:13,340
Even experts touch baskets and door handles.

70
00:04:15,250 --> 00:04:19,730
These are fresh lemon samples. A small taste
is enough.

71
00:04:19,880 --> 00:04:21,280
You first, Dad.

72
00:04:21,430 --> 00:04:24,150
A fair request from my assistant.

73
00:04:24,300 --> 00:04:28,340
I'm watching your eyebrows. They always give
you away.

74
00:04:30,250 --> 00:04:33,850
Oh! That is definitely not a tiny orange.

75
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:35,840
Your face folded up!

76
00:04:35,990 --> 00:04:38,710
It discovered a very powerful lemon.

77
00:04:38,860 --> 00:04:43,340
Those eyebrows just told us exactly how strong
it is.

78
00:04:45,250 --> 00:04:48,410
If it is sour, is it spoiled?

79
00:04:48,560 --> 00:04:51,280
No. Fresh lemons are naturally sour.

80
00:04:51,430 --> 00:04:54,590
My surprise was bigger than the sample.

81
00:04:54,740 --> 00:04:58,780
Your nose certainly had something to say about
it.

82
00:05:00,250 --> 00:05:03,952
Sour describes this lemon’s taste. Spoiled
food is different.

83
00:05:04,102 --> 00:05:08,208
We never taste food to check whether it is
spoiled.

84
00:05:08,358 --> 00:05:10,448
I will skip mine today.

85
00:05:10,598 --> 00:05:14,300
That's fine, Max. You can smell the orange
instead.

86
00:05:15,250 --> 00:05:17,819
This orange is sweet and juicy.

87
00:05:17,969 --> 00:05:21,369
And the lemon was sour. Both were fresh.

88
00:05:21,519 --> 00:05:24,919
So their names were not the only difference.

89
00:05:25,069 --> 00:05:29,300
My eyebrows prefer this one. Did you see them
relax?

90
00:05:30,250 --> 00:05:32,530
Sweet. And a little tangy.

91
00:05:32,680 --> 00:05:34,960
That is a useful description.

92
00:05:35,110 --> 00:05:39,150
Better than saying it tastes like a yellow
circle.

93
00:05:39,300 --> 00:05:43,340
I'd know exactly which sample you meant from
that.

94
00:05:45,250 --> 00:05:50,610
New rule. We choose by what we need, not what
looks impressive.

95
00:05:50,760 --> 00:05:52,160
That sounds promising.

96
00:05:52,310 --> 00:05:55,030
Let us start with two apples.

97
00:05:55,180 --> 00:05:59,220
I'll help you find a pair that looks good.

98
00:06:00,250 --> 00:06:03,650
These apples are firm, with no damaged spots.

99
00:06:03,800 --> 00:06:06,784
Even though they are not the biggest?

100
00:06:06,934 --> 00:06:09,919
They are exactly the size we need.

101
00:06:10,069 --> 00:06:14,300
Then those two get the first seats in the
basket.

102
00:06:15,250 --> 00:06:19,290
Two apples, one dollar each. Two dollars so
far.

103
00:06:19,440 --> 00:06:20,840
A small start.

104
00:06:20,990 --> 00:06:24,150
A good start. There is a difference.

105
00:06:24,300 --> 00:06:27,900
And fourteen dollars still gives us choices,
Dad.

106
00:06:30,250 --> 00:06:34,290
One, two, three, four. Four bananas in one
bunch.

107
00:06:34,440 --> 00:06:36,280
A very well-organized family.

108
00:06:36,430 --> 00:06:39,590
And ripe enough for our salad today.

109
00:06:39,740 --> 00:06:44,220
Did they have to hold hands all the way here?

110
00:06:45,250 --> 00:06:49,290
The whole bunch is two dollars. Four dollars
altogether.

111
00:06:49,440 --> 00:06:50,840
No extra passengers.

112
00:06:50,990 --> 00:06:54,150
Good. We have more fruit to invite.

113
00:06:54,300 --> 00:06:58,340
Who gets the next invitation? Something round,
I hope.

114
00:07:00,250 --> 00:07:03,850
Two oranges. I checked the name this time.

115
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:05,400
Seventy-five cents each.

116
00:07:05,550 --> 00:07:09,590
One fifty for both. Our total is five fifty.

117
00:07:09,740 --> 00:07:13,780
Two oranges, one price each. I'm following the
total.

118
00:07:15,250 --> 00:07:18,234
Why buy a lemon after that surprise?

119
00:07:18,384 --> 00:07:21,784
A little juice can brighten the sweet fruit.

120
00:07:21,934 --> 00:07:24,919
A little sounds better than a mouthful.

121
00:07:25,069 --> 00:07:29,300
Just enough for the juice to reach all the
fruit.

122
00:07:30,250 --> 00:07:33,650
One lemon. One lime. Different names,
different jobs.

123
00:07:33,800 --> 00:07:36,369
Fifty cents each. Six fifty now.

124
00:07:36,519 --> 00:07:40,334
And this time we know what we are choosing.

125
00:07:40,484 --> 00:07:44,300
Good. My mouth remembers which one it met
first.

126
00:07:45,250 --> 00:07:47,743
How many grapes make a pound?

127
00:07:47,893 --> 00:07:51,998
It depends on their size. The scale measures
the weight.

128
00:07:52,148 --> 00:07:54,641
One pound is enough for us.

129
00:07:54,791 --> 00:07:59,300
I'm glad you said weight. I'd be here counting
all day.

130
00:08:00,250 --> 00:08:04,614
One pound at three dollars a pound. Nine fifty
altogether.

131
00:08:04,764 --> 00:08:07,843
The scale is a useful shopping partner.

132
00:08:07,993 --> 00:08:10,214
It gives very precise advice.

133
00:08:10,364 --> 00:08:14,300
And it doesn't care how impressive a grape
looks.

134
00:08:15,250 --> 00:08:19,066
These strawberries look good, and the lid
protects them.

135
00:08:19,216 --> 00:08:20,953
One pound, three dollars.

136
00:08:21,103 --> 00:08:25,334
Twelve fifty now. We still have room in the
budget.

137
00:08:25,484 --> 00:08:29,300
Can we keep the lid clear when we pack?

138
00:08:30,250 --> 00:08:33,234
The biggest fruit should go on top.

139
00:08:33,384 --> 00:08:35,953
The soft fruit should stay protected.

140
00:08:36,103 --> 00:08:39,919
Right. My old rule nearly squashed our new
plan.

141
00:08:40,069 --> 00:08:44,300
The berries would like to thank you for
changing it.

142
00:08:45,250 --> 00:08:47,970
Should we take the firmest peaches?

143
00:08:48,120 --> 00:08:51,280
These are ripe for today. Gentle hands.

144
00:08:51,430 --> 00:08:55,030
They smell lovely. Let us choose these two.

145
00:08:55,180 --> 00:08:59,220
I can hold the basket steady while you choose.

146
00:09:00,250 --> 00:09:03,329
Two peaches at seventy-five cents. Fourteen
dollars.

147
00:09:03,479 --> 00:09:05,700
They get the gentle seats.

148
00:09:05,850 --> 00:09:10,214
Good thinking. Soft fruit travels better with
a little care.

149
00:09:10,364 --> 00:09:14,300
I'll leave their spot clear while you finish
counting.

150
00:09:15,250 --> 00:09:17,403
These two fit our hands.

151
00:09:17,553 --> 00:09:19,291
And the biggest one?

152
00:09:19,441 --> 00:09:25,750
We need two ripe pears, not a pear contest.
These two are a good team.

153
00:09:25,900 --> 00:09:29,300
My hands vote for the little team, too.

154
00:09:30,250 --> 00:09:33,410
Two pears, one fifty. Fifteen fifty
altogether.

155
00:09:33,560 --> 00:09:34,520
Under sixteen?

156
00:09:34,670 --> 00:09:39,150
Yes. And every fruit has a reason to be here.

157
00:09:39,300 --> 00:09:43,340
Just under. That's close enough to check it
twice.

158
00:09:45,250 --> 00:09:49,290
We have everything for the salad, and it fits.

159
00:09:49,440 --> 00:09:52,600
The basket did not even need confidence.

160
00:09:52,750 --> 00:09:54,590
Just a sensible plan.

161
00:09:54,740 --> 00:09:58,780
The melon can stay on its very own shelf.

162
00:10:00,250 --> 00:10:06,490
Two, two, one fifty, fifty cents, fifty cents,
three, three, one fifty, one fifty.

163
00:10:06,640 --> 00:10:09,800
Fifteen fifty. That is our actual total.

164
00:10:09,950 --> 00:10:11,790
Under our limit, then?

165
00:10:11,940 --> 00:10:13,340
By fifty cents.

166
00:10:15,250 --> 00:10:18,650
So we have fifty cents left to spend.

167
00:10:18,800 --> 00:10:23,447
Yes. Not four fifty. That will be the change
from twenty.

168
00:10:23,597 --> 00:10:24,919
Different starting amounts.

169
00:10:25,069 --> 00:10:29,300
Ah, the change starts with the twenty in my
wallet.

170
00:10:30,250 --> 00:10:32,970
What if we added the watermelon?

171
00:10:33,120 --> 00:10:36,280
Twenty-three fifty. Seven fifty over our
limit.

172
00:10:36,430 --> 00:10:39,590
And one whole basket over our basket.

173
00:10:39,740 --> 00:10:43,780
I don't think my elbows can afford that
either.

174
00:10:45,250 --> 00:10:50,312
No watermelon today. Bigger would leave us
with less of everything else.

175
00:10:50,462 --> 00:10:52,616
That is a thoughtful choice.

176
00:10:52,766 --> 00:10:55,334
I am trying a new specialty.

177
00:10:55,484 --> 00:10:59,300
Choosing on purpose? You might get good at
that.

178
00:11:00,250 --> 00:11:03,850
Will the whole salad make that face happen?

179
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:06,280
I was wondering that too.

180
00:11:06,430 --> 00:11:09,590
We will use just a little juice.

181
00:11:09,740 --> 00:11:14,220
I'll watch the spoon. My cheeks are counting
on you.

182
00:11:15,250 --> 00:11:19,897
Half a lemon and half a lime for the whole
bowl.

183
00:11:20,047 --> 00:11:22,616
Not a bowl full of lemons?

184
00:11:22,766 --> 00:11:24,919
I will help keep track.

185
00:11:25,069 --> 00:11:29,300
Just wait until you smell the orange in there,
too.

186
00:11:30,250 --> 00:11:33,757
I will cut the apples, pears and peaches.

187
00:11:33,907 --> 00:11:36,986
I will prepare the grapes and dressing.

188
00:11:37,136 --> 00:11:40,214
We can set out the picnic things.

189
00:11:40,364 --> 00:11:44,300
Sounds like the picnic crew is already on
duty.

190
00:11:45,250 --> 00:11:50,170
We have fruit for everyone, and a plan for
every fruit.

191
00:11:50,320 --> 00:11:53,480
That sounds more useful than a champion.

192
00:11:53,630 --> 00:11:55,470
And easier to carry.

193
00:11:55,620 --> 00:11:59,220
Easier to share, too. Everybody helped pick
something.

194
00:12:00,250 --> 00:12:03,298
Fifteen fifty. It matches our card.

195
00:12:03,448 --> 00:12:06,003
Good. We checked before guessing.

196
00:12:06,153 --> 00:12:07,722
And before paying.

197
00:12:07,872 --> 00:12:12,400
That makes me feel better about handing over
twenty.

198
00:12:15,250 --> 00:12:17,530
Here you go. Twenty dollars.

199
00:12:17,680 --> 00:12:21,720
We should get four dollars and fifty cents
back.

200
00:12:21,870 --> 00:12:24,150
I will check that too.

201
00:12:24,300 --> 00:12:28,340
Four fifty back. I'll watch while you count
it.

202
00:12:30,250 --> 00:12:33,410
Four dollars, and two quarters. Four fifty.

203
00:12:33,560 --> 00:12:36,720
That is more than our fifty cents.

204
00:12:36,870 --> 00:12:39,150
Yes. Let us check why.

205
00:12:39,300 --> 00:12:43,340
Let's start with the bill I actually handed
over.

206
00:12:45,250 --> 00:12:49,759
Twenty paid gives four fifty back. Sixteen
budgeted leaves fifty cents.

207
00:12:49,909 --> 00:12:52,805
So four dollars were never picnic money.

208
00:12:52,955 --> 00:12:55,448
Exactly. Every dollar keeps its job.

209
00:12:55,598 --> 00:12:59,300
Even the ones that never wanted to go
shopping.

210
00:13:00,250 --> 00:13:03,850
The soft fruit can travel in the cooler.

211
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:05,400
Nothing gets squashed.

212
00:13:05,550 --> 00:13:09,590
A much better journey than riding under a
watermelon.

213
00:13:09,740 --> 00:13:14,220
I can give the soft ones a little elbow room.

214
00:13:15,250 --> 00:13:17,530
Fruit, receipt, change. All checked.

215
00:13:17,680 --> 00:13:19,080
And the blanket.

216
00:13:19,230 --> 00:13:24,150
I would hate to tell lunch it had to stand up.

217
00:13:24,300 --> 00:13:28,780
And lunch has six people who would like to
sit.

218
00:13:30,250 --> 00:13:32,090
Happy with our choices?

219
00:13:32,240 --> 00:13:36,280
Very. We bought a picnic, not a size record.

220
00:13:36,430 --> 00:13:39,590
And we left the giant table behind.

221
00:13:39,740 --> 00:13:43,780
That giant table still had an eight-dollar
price tag.

222
00:13:45,250 --> 00:13:47,970
All fruit delivered. No champion required.

223
00:13:48,120 --> 00:13:49,960
Now we prepare it.

224
00:13:50,110 --> 00:13:53,270
Can we make the lemon less surprising?

225
00:13:53,420 --> 00:13:58,340
Wait until it's mixed in. Then tell me what
you taste.

226
00:14:00,250 --> 00:14:02,530
Hands first, after our trip.

227
00:14:02,680 --> 00:14:04,080
Then the fruit?

228
00:14:04,230 --> 00:14:09,590
Yes. Clean hands, clean fruit, clean plates.
We all have a job.

229
00:14:09,740 --> 00:14:13,780
Good. I'll keep the plates clear while we're
washing.

230
00:14:15,250 --> 00:14:17,970
Even the lemon peel gets washed?

231
00:14:18,120 --> 00:14:20,400
Yes, before we cut it.

232
00:14:20,550 --> 00:14:23,710
Rinse, drain, then prepare. We have time.

233
00:14:23,860 --> 00:14:28,340
I can wait. I like the way that peel smells.

234
00:14:30,250 --> 00:14:33,952
We can put out the plates while you cut.

235
00:14:34,102 --> 00:14:36,998
Thank you. I will handle the knife.

236
00:14:37,148 --> 00:14:40,044
And I will escort these pits away.

237
00:14:40,194 --> 00:14:44,300
Thank you. A clear counter gives me room to
work.

238
00:14:45,250 --> 00:14:47,900
Four little pieces from one grape?

239
00:14:48,050 --> 00:14:51,557
Yes, cut lengthwise. Small pieces for our
salad.

240
00:14:51,707 --> 00:14:55,214
These grapes are traveling in a group too.

241
00:14:55,364 --> 00:14:59,300
Little boats. I can see all four of them.

242
00:15:00,250 --> 00:15:04,356
The red strawberries make it look like a
picnic already.

243
00:15:04,506 --> 00:15:07,805
And every color came from our own choices.

244
00:15:07,955 --> 00:15:10,044
One bowl, plenty to share.

245
00:15:10,194 --> 00:15:14,300
We can spot the red ones from across the
island.

246
00:15:15,250 --> 00:15:18,410
Two oranges. Round, sweet, and correctly
introduced.

247
00:15:18,560 --> 00:15:20,840
No folded-up face this time.

248
00:15:20,990 --> 00:15:24,590
Please save the face until after the peeling.

249
00:15:24,740 --> 00:15:28,780
My face will wait. My hands are busy anyway.

250
00:15:30,250 --> 00:15:32,530
Four bananas. One empty bunch.

251
00:15:32,680 --> 00:15:35,840
Their family meeting moved into the bowl.

252
00:15:35,990 --> 00:15:39,590
Now for a little lemon and lime juice.

253
00:15:39,740 --> 00:15:44,220
Remember my lemon face? Let's give it a
smaller job.

254
00:15:45,250 --> 00:15:49,290
Half a lemon. Half a lime. I am checking.

255
00:15:49,440 --> 00:15:52,600
The little job for the strong flavor.

256
00:15:52,750 --> 00:15:55,030
Exactly. No guessing this time.

257
00:15:55,180 --> 00:15:59,220
Good. My tongue would like a little warning
today.

258
00:16:00,250 --> 00:16:02,090
We are saving those?

259
00:16:02,240 --> 00:16:05,400
Yes. Wrapped, covered and in the fridge.

260
00:16:05,550 --> 00:16:08,710
A little today, a little another day.

261
00:16:08,860 --> 00:16:12,900
I like that we didn't need a whole one.

262
00:16:15,250 --> 00:16:18,329
A little sour with all this sweet.

263
00:16:18,479 --> 00:16:21,128
That is the balance we planned.

264
00:16:21,278 --> 00:16:25,643
I can decide what I think when we sit down.

265
00:16:25,793 --> 00:16:29,300
Deal. Tell me which flavor you notice first.

266
00:16:30,250 --> 00:16:33,850
Lid on. Into the cooler until we eat.

267
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:35,840
Forks in the basket.

268
00:16:35,990 --> 00:16:38,710
Now our short journey can begin.

269
00:16:38,860 --> 00:16:42,900
Luckily the shade is only a few steps away.

270
00:16:45,250 --> 00:16:47,530
Lunch has reached its destination.

271
00:16:47,680 --> 00:16:49,520
No bus ticket required.

272
00:16:49,670 --> 00:16:54,150
And the tree has kept our seats in the shade.

273
00:16:54,300 --> 00:16:59,220
Best trip yet. Nobody had to check the price
of shade.

274
00:17:00,250 --> 00:17:02,043
Six places. Everyone here.

275
00:17:02,193 --> 00:17:05,700
And nobody has to sit on the watermelon.

276
00:17:05,850 --> 00:17:08,928
A relief for the watermelon as well.

277
00:17:09,078 --> 00:17:14,300
I think that melon would have rolled right out
from under me.

278
00:17:15,250 --> 00:17:17,530
Fruit salad, ready at last.

279
00:17:17,680 --> 00:17:20,400
It smells like oranges and strawberries.

280
00:17:20,550 --> 00:17:24,150
A much friendlier introduction than my lemon
sample.

281
00:17:24,300 --> 00:17:28,780
Give it a sniff before you pick your first
bite.

282
00:17:30,250 --> 00:17:32,970
Sweet first. Then a little tangy.

283
00:17:33,120 --> 00:17:34,960
Do you like it?

284
00:17:35,110 --> 00:17:38,710
I do. The lemon has learned to share.

285
00:17:38,860 --> 00:17:43,340
I wish it'd learned that before I met the
sample.

286
00:17:45,250 --> 00:17:50,900
I wanted the biggest fruit because I wanted
this picnic to feel special.

287
00:17:51,050 --> 00:17:51,986
It does.

288
00:17:52,136 --> 00:17:55,214
You did not need to impress us.

289
00:17:55,364 --> 00:17:59,300
Oh. I could've asked what felt special to you.

290
00:18:00,250 --> 00:18:03,971
We all came. That was the special part.

291
00:18:04,121 --> 00:18:06,023
Even before the fruit?

292
00:18:06,173 --> 00:18:08,074
Especially before the fruit.

293
00:18:08,224 --> 00:18:12,400
I'd still come sit under the tree with you.

294
00:18:15,250 --> 00:18:17,970
No fruit won the size contest.

295
00:18:18,120 --> 00:18:20,840
They made a good team instead.

296
00:18:20,990 --> 00:18:24,150
I am happy to join the team.

297
00:18:24,300 --> 00:18:29,220
Good. There's room under this tree for all six
of us.

298
00:18:30,250 --> 00:18:33,410
The samples helped us understand the
difference.

299
00:18:33,560 --> 00:18:38,040
And your careful choosing helped us bring good
fruit home.

300
00:18:38,190 --> 00:18:40,030
I had excellent company.

301
00:18:40,180 --> 00:18:43,780
You made picking two little pears feel
important.

302
00:18:45,250 --> 00:18:47,970
Dad, your face is staying unfolded.

303
00:18:48,120 --> 00:18:49,960
A successful picnic result.

304
00:18:50,110 --> 00:18:52,830
Yes. All my corners are comfortable.

305
00:18:52,980 --> 00:18:57,460
Then I guess we gave the lemon just enough
work.

306
00:19:00,250 --> 00:19:02,090
We stayed under sixteen.

307
00:19:02,240 --> 00:19:04,520
And brought everything we needed.

308
00:19:04,670 --> 00:19:08,710
One list, two colors, six people who actually
listened.

309
00:19:08,860 --> 00:19:12,900
And nobody had to carry a picnic table home.

310
00:19:15,250 --> 00:19:17,471
Maybe pancakes for breakfast tomorrow.

311
00:19:17,621 --> 00:19:21,557
I would like to help. After lunch, of course.

312
00:19:21,707 --> 00:19:24,786
Good. We will make a plan together.

313
00:19:24,936 --> 00:19:29,300
If it's breakfast, I vote for something we can
flip.

314
00:19:30,250 --> 00:19:33,850
The basket carried fruit out and dishes back.

315
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:35,840
A useful second job.

316
00:19:35,990 --> 00:19:39,150
And the rest stays cool for later.

317
00:19:39,300 --> 00:19:43,340
Let's carry the dishes level on the way
inside.

318
00:19:45,250 --> 00:19:48,757
Not the biggest fruit. A very happy picnic.

319
00:19:48,907 --> 00:19:51,128
Exactly the picnic we needed.

320
00:19:51,278 --> 00:19:54,786
And the basket can keep its small door.

321
00:19:54,936 --> 00:19:59,300
Tomorrow's breakfast can stay home. My feet
vote for that.
